WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.298
The first mistake in time management is assuming that a time block is useful just because it is empty.

2
00:00:06.298 --> 00:00:11.546
In reality, attention and energy determine what kind of work fits inside the block.

3
00:00:11.546 --> 00:00:22.043
A half hour between meetings may be fine for administrative cleanup, but poor for architectural thinking, writing, code review, study, or a sensitive conversation.

4
00:00:22.043 --> 00:00:26.617
The same number of minutes can have very different productive capacity.

5
00:00:26.617 --> 00:00:33.739
Deep work requires sustained attention, a stable context, and enough cognitive room to hold the problem in mind.

6
00:00:33.739 --> 00:00:40.712
Shallow work is not bad; messages, approvals, scheduling, and routine updates often keep operations moving.

7
00:00:40.712 --> 00:00:45.586
But shallow work becomes expensive when it constantly interrupts deep work.

8
00:00:45.586 --> 00:00:52.933
Each context switch forces attention to reload the task, reconstruct decisions, and recover the thread of thought.

9
00:00:52.933 --> 00:00:56.832
Cognitive load is the mental demand placed on working memory.

10
00:00:56.832 --> 00:01:02.455
Fatigue, ambiguity, noise, interruptions, and emotional pressure all raise that load.

11
00:01:02.455 --> 00:01:08.078
When load is high, people may still push through, but accuracy and judgment often suffer.

12
00:01:08.078 --> 00:01:11.377
A time problem may actually be an attention problem.

13
00:01:11.377 --> 00:01:18.725
It may also be an energy problem, where the person has hours available but not enough recovery to use those hours well.

14
00:01:18.725 --> 00:01:22.324
A practical system matches task type to available state.

15
00:01:22.324 --> 00:01:25.848
High-consequence work deserves protected attention.

16
00:01:25.848 --> 00:01:29.372
Routine work can be grouped into lower-energy periods.

17
00:01:29.372 --> 00:01:35.670
Recovery should be treated as an input to performance, not as a reward for having already performed.

18
00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:43.767
When planning accounts for attention and energy, the calendar becomes more realistic and less likely to create false promises.
